    Generate performance.json without a global venv
    
    The capture-performance-data role always calls setup_devstack_virtualenv.
    Where GLOBAL_VENV is False, as on CentOS Stream and Rocky, there is no
    venv: the function tries to create one with an unset $PYTHON, then runs
    get-stats.py from a venv that does not exist, leaving an empty file.
    
    Use the global venv's python when it exists, and otherwise the python
    that devstack installed packages with, so these jobs report again.
    
    Prompt: Mikal asked for a fix for the empty performance.json on CentOS
    and Rocky jobs, found while checking the new privsep memory telemetry.
    
    Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (low effort)
    
    Change-Id: I3b786c3e3bc8c0da616f7f09f506672aadf72543
    Signed-off-by: Michael Still <mikal@stillhq.com>

diff --git a/roles/capture-performance-data/tasks/main.yaml b/roles/capture-performance-data/tasks/main.yaml
index 51a11b60..c0942997 100644
--- a/roles/capture-performance-data/tasks/main.yaml
+++ b/roles/capture-performance-data/tasks/main.yaml
@@ -3,8 +3,13 @@
     executable: /bin/bash
     cmd: |
       source {{ devstack_conf_dir }}/stackrc
-      source {{ devstack_conf_dir }}/inc/python
-      setup_devstack_virtualenv
+      # There is no global venv where GLOBAL_VENV is False, such as on
+      # CentOS Stream and Rocky; packages are in the system python there.
+      if [[ -d $DEVSTACK_VENV ]]; then
+          PYTHON=$DEVSTACK_VENV/bin/python3
+      else
+          PYTHON=$(which python${PYTHON3_VERSION})
+      fi
       $PYTHON {{ devstack_conf_dir }}/tools/get-stats.py \
         --db-user="$DATABASE_USER" \
         --db-pass="$DATABASE_PASSWORD" \
